# What happened when markets were allowed in?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What happened to farm output when China let households keep their surplus after 1978? What does one case prove?"
next: {"title":"Market failure or government failure — which is worse?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/67-market-failure-or-government-failure.cast"}

## One village, one country
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1970
      - 1971
      - 1972
      - 1973
      - 1974
      - 1975
      - 1976
      - 1977
      - 1978
      - 1979
      - 1980
      - 1981
      - 1982
      - 1983
      - 1984
    series:
      - {name: China, values: [197.6, 209.1, 203.3, 218.9, 231.4, 241.2, 246.7, 239.8, 269.8, 289.5, 277.2, 283.3, 312.1, 342.3, 362.8]}
      - {name: team era, values: [197.6, 209.1, 203.3, 218.9, 231.4, 241.2, 246.7, 239.8, 269.8, null, null, null, null, null, null]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 450
    x_label: Year
    y_label: Cereal harvest, million tonnes
    label_size: 22
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 190
      w: 520
      h: 400
sources:
    - id: fao_cereals
      title: Cereal production (FAO, via Our World in Data)
      authors: FAO; Our World in Data
      year: 2024
      finding: 'China''s cereal harvest: 198 Mt (1970), 270 Mt (1978), 363 Mt (1984); harvested cereal area fell from about 96.6 to 91.7 million ha (production / yield), about 5% less'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/cereal-production
    - id: xiaogang
      title: The Xiaogang experiment (The World of Chinese, 2020); NPR interview with Yan Hongchang (2011)
      authors: The World of Chinese; NPR
      year: 2020
      finding: Late 1978, Xiaogang farmers secretly signed a pact to farm family plots, meet the state quota and keep the surplus; if leaders were jailed the others would raise their children; villagers say the 1979 harvest beat the previous five years combined
    - id: lin87
      title: 'The household responsibility system reform in China: a peasant''s institutional choice'
      authors: Justin Yifu Lin
      year: 1987
      finding: 'Share of production teams on the household responsibility system: about 1% end-1979, 45% end-1981, about 98% end-1983'

    dot p1970 at.data [1970,197.6] cites ["fao_cereals"] hidden true
    label p1970_label "198" above attach_to p1970 hidden true
    dot p1978 at.data [1978,269.8] hidden true
    label p1978_label "270" above-left attach_to p1978 hidden true
    scratch pact cites ["xiaogang"] work ["Xiaogang, late 1978","land split by family","quota delivered","the rest kept","jailed? we raise your kids","1979 crop > prior 5 years"] x 805 y 420 font_size 25 hidden true
    scratch adopt cites ["lin87"] work ["household system","1979: 1% of teams","1981: 45%","1983: 98%"] x 805 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p1984 at.data [1984,362.8] cites ["fao_cereals"] hidden true
    label p1984_label "363" above-left attach_to p1984 hidden true
    text jump "+34%" #b5482e x 800 y 470 font_size 72 hidden true

    card title "What happened when markets were allowed in?"

What happens to a harvest when farmers may keep whatever they grow beyond the state's quota? China found out after 1978.
    draw axes

B: Until then, its farmland was worked by teams of families. The harve (@highlight line_2@)st was pooled, and shared out by work points.
    draw line_2

And the cereal harvest grew, slowly: from (@draw p1970 p1970_label@) about two hundred million tonnes in 1970 to two hund (@draw p1978 p1978_label@)red and seventy in 1978.

B: Then, late in 1978, in Xiaogang, a poor village in Anhui province, eighteen farmers signed a secret pact.
    draw pact_box pact_line_1

Spli (@draw pact_line_2@)t the land among the families. (@draw pact_line_3@) Deliver the state's quota. (@draw pact_line_4@) Keep the rest.

B: And if the leaders went to jail  (@draw pact_line_5@)for it, the others would raise their children. That's how illegal it was.

By the village's own account, its ne (@draw pact_line_6@)xt harvest beat the previous five years put together.

    erase pact

B: Beijing first tolerated it, then backed it. At t (@draw adopt_box adopt_line_1 adopt_line_2@)he end of 1979, one team in a hundr (@draw adopt_line_3@)ed farmed this way; by 1983, ninet (@draw adopt_line_4@)y-eight in a hundred.

B: My guess for the whole country: a bump, maybe ten percent, then back to the old trend.
    erase adopt line_2

    ask "Draw China's cereal harvest on, from 1978 to 1984." on line_1 from 1979 store g relative true
        right: Close: on average you were {g.pct} off.
        wrong: On average you were {g.pct} off. Here is the real line.

Up by a third: from two hundred and seventy to (@draw p1984 p1984_label@) three hundred and sixty-three millio (@draw jump@)n tonnes.

B: On about five percent less land.
    text land "on ~5% less land" #3d3833 x 800 y 400 font_size 30

So the grain came. Was it the pact that brought it, and what can one case like this prove?
    highlight line_1

    pause 0.5

## The last sack
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - y = s*(60 - 5*x)
      - y = 2 + 2*x
    params:
      s: {value: 1, min: 0.05, max: 1, step: 0.05, label: share kept s}
    drag: s
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 60
    x_label: care in the field (hours)
    y_label: kilos, per extra hour
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: (60*s - 2)/(5*s + 2), curve: 1, label: false, id: effort}

    text illus "illustrative numbers" #8f887c x 250 y 520 font_size 22 hidden true
    label effort_lab "effort" above #3d3833 attach_to effort font_size 26 hidden true
    text lab_keep "— what you keep" #2f6b8f x 250 y 610 font_size 28 hidden true
    text lab_cost "— what the hour costs" #b5482e x 250 y 565 font_size 28 hidden true
    text share "share kept: {eq.s:2}" x 780 y 610 font_size 36 hidden true
    text cue_days "days recorded, not care" x 780 y 555 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cue_quota "quota: off the first sacks" x 780 y 555 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch sc work [{"tex":"\\text{grain } f(e),\\ \\text{share } s,\\ \\text{cost } c(e)"},{"tex":"\\max_e\\ s\\,f(e) - c(e)"},{"tex":"s\\,\\Delta f = \\Delta c"},{"tex":"s = \\tfrac{1}{20}:\\ \\Delta f = 20\\,\\Delta c"}] x 560 y 360 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "The last sack"

B: A third more grain, from the same land and the same farmers. Where did it come from?
    draw axes illus

Start with one more hour of care in the field: weeding, watching the water. It grows a litt (@highlight curve_0@)le more grain, and each extra hour a little less.
    draw curve_0 lab_keep

B: And each hour costs you more: you're tired, and there's a pig to feed at home.
    draw curve_1 lab_cost

A family that keeps every extra kilo works until the two meet.
    draw effort effort_lab

B: But in a team the harvest is pooled. Say twenty famil (@draw share@)ies: every extra sack you grow is split twenty ways.

    erase effort_lab

So the gain you feel shrinks to a twentieth, and the point where an ho (@draw effort_lab@)ur pays slides almost to zero.
    animate params.s 0.05 duration 3 ghost true

B: Teams fought back: leaders watched, and work points paid for days in th (@draw cue_days@)e field. But a day recorded isn't a day of care.

    erase cue_days

Now the pact. The family still owes its fixed quota (@draw cue_quota@), but that comes off the first sacks, not the last.

B: The last sack is all theirs again, so the effort comes back. Same land, same people.
    animate params.s 1 duration 3

    erase cue_quota

Try it: drag the blue line. Try a share of one half, what a sharecropper keeps, and see how hard it pays to work.
    explore

    erase equations

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the same thing in symbols?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes curves effort effort_lab lab_keep lab_cost share illus to 0.12

Effort e grows f of e kilos; the family keeps a share s, and the effort costs it c of e.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

It picks e to make s times f of e, minus c of e, as large as it can.
    draw sc_line_2

That's where s times the last hour's grain equals the last hour's cost.
    draw sc_line_3

With s at one twentieth, an hour must grow twenty times its cost before it pays.
    draw sc_line_4

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes curves effort effort_lab lab_keep lab_cost share illus to 1

So the reform didn't make anyone a better farmer. It gave them back the last sack.
    highlight effort

    pause 0.5

## What one case proves
