sources:
    - id: fao_cereals
      title: Cereal production (FAO, via Our World in Data)
      authors: FAO; Our World in Data
      year: 2024
      finding: China's cereal harvest 363 Mt in 1984, 337 Mt in 1985, below 1984 until 1989 (365 Mt)
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/cereal-production
    - id: fao_n
      title: Nitrogen fertilizer use per area of cropland (FAO, via Our World in Data)
      authors: FAO; Our World in Data
      year: 2024
      finding: 'China: 93 kg/ha (1978) to 129 kg/ha (1984), +38%'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/nitrogen-fertilizer-application-per-hectare-of-cropland
    - id: prices79
      title: Incentive effects of price rises and payment-system changes on Chinese agricultural productivity growth (NBER WP 2148); Sicular on 1979 procurement prices
      authors: McMillan, J., Whalley, J. & Zhu, L.
      year: 1987
      finding: '1979: quota prices for grain raised about 20%; above-quota premium raised from 30% to 50%'
    - id: lin92
      title: Rural Reforms and Agricultural Growth in China
      authors: Justin Yifu Lin
      year: 1992
      finding: 'Province-level panel 1970-87: decollectivization (the household responsibility system) accounts for about half of output growth 1978-84; price adjustments mattered mainly through input use; growth slowed after 1984'
      url: https://ideas.repec.org/a/aea/aecrev/v82y1992i1p34-51.html
    - id: mwz89
      title: The Impact of China's Economic Reforms on Agricultural Productivity Growth
      authors: McMillan, J., Whalley, J. & Zhu, L.
      year: 1989
      finding: About three-quarters of the 1978-84 productivity gain attributed to the responsibility system's incentives, about one-quarter to higher prices (Journal of Political Economy 97(4))
    - id: lin87
      title: 'The household responsibility system reform in China: a peasant''s institutional choice'
      authors: Justin Yifu Lin
      year: 1987
      finding: The household system was adopted first in poorer areas
    - id: vietnam
      title: 'Vietnam: Decollectivization and Rice Productivity Growth'
      authors: Pingali, P. & Xuan, V.-T.
      year: 1992
      finding: Vietnam re-emerged as a rice exporter in 1989 after two decades as a net importer, following decollectivization (Directive 100 of 1981, Resolution 10 of 1988)
    - id: lazear
      title: Performance Pay and Productivity
      authors: Edward P. Lazear
      year: 2000
      finding: Safelite's switch from hourly wages to piece rates raised output per worker 44%; about half from the average worker producing more, the rest from hiring and keeping more productive workers (AER 90(5))

    scratch also cites ["prices79","fao_n"] work ["also from 1979:","quota price +20%","above-quota bonus 30% → 50%","nitrogen per hectare +38%"] x 410 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait lin x 870 y 430 width 190 of "Justin Yifu Lin" cameo true hidden true
    text timing "provinces switched in different years" x 410 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true
    text half "household system: ≈ ½" #b5482e cites ["lin92"] x 410 y 390 font_size 46 hidden true
    text half2 "of 1978–84 growth (Lin)" #b5482e x 410 y 345 font_size 32 hidden true
    text three "McMillan et al.: ≈ ¾" #2f6b8f cites ["mwz89"] x 410 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    text three2 "of the productivity gain" #2f6b8f x 410 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    dot p1984 at.data [1984,362.8] hidden true
    path peak dashed points [[538,528],[730,528]] style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    text jump "one-time jump" x 640 y 568 font_size 30 hidden true
    text agreed "raised output: mostly agreed" #4f7f3a x 420 y 350 font_size 32 hidden true
    text close "keep the last sack → more grain" x 420 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text contested "how much: contested" #b5482e x 420 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "What one case proves"

B: That's the theory. But in 1979 a lot more changed than who kept the last sack.
    draw axes line_1

    fade axes line_1 to 0.15

The state rais (@draw also_box also_line_1 also_line_2@)ed what it paid for quota grain by about a fifth, and the bonus for g (@draw also_line_3@)rain sold above the quota from thirty to fifty percent.

B: Farmers were spreading more fertilis (@draw also_line_4@)er too: over a third more nitrogen per hectare by 1984.

And the first villages to switch weren (@erase also@)'t chosen at random. They were poor places like Xiaogang, where the teams had done worst.

The economist Ju (@draw lin@)stin Lin used timing to pull these apart. Provinces switched to household far (@draw timing@)ming in different years; the price rises hit them all at once.

    erase timing

Comparing early and late switchers, he credite (@draw half half2@)d the household system with about half the growth from 1978 to 1984.

    erase lin

B: Another study, by McMillan, Whalley and Z (@draw three three2@)hu, gave it about three-quarters of the gain in productivity.

    erase half half2 three three2

    fade axes line_1 to 1

Now look  (@draw p1984@)past 1984. The harvest dro (@animate stage 1 duration 3@)pped, and stayed below its 1984 peak for four years.

B: That fits the story. Handing back the last sack  (@draw peak jump@)is a one-time jump to a higher level, not a faster growth rate forever.

    erase jump peak

How sure are we? That the household sys (@draw agreed@)tem raised output a lot: mostly agreed. How much, against p (@draw contested@)rices and fertiliser: contested.

B: And what it proves is narrow. The land stayed collective, and the quota was still sold at state prices. It shows what's lost when nobody keeps the last sack, not that markets fix everything.
    highlight contested

    erase agreed contested

    quiz "Vietnam gave farm households land contracts in 1988 and exported rice in 1989, after two decades as a net importer. What does that show on its own?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: It fits, but on its own it can't separate the contracts from everything else that changed at the same time.
        wrong: What did it take to pull China's contracts apart from its price rises? Timing that varied by place: here, it fits, but the other changes must be checked.
        * It proves the contracts worked
        + It fits, but what else changed and where reform came first must be checked
        * Land contracts never matter

    quiz "Two fishing boats. On one, the crew splits half the value of the catch; on the other, each deckhand gets a flat day wage. Which crew keeps hunting for fish late on a slow day?" voice b
        right: The share crew: every extra fish puts money in their pockets, while the day wage pays the same whatever the hold holds.
        wrong: Who gets anything from the last fish? Only those paid by the catch: here, the share crew.
        + The share crew
        * The wage crew
        * Both the same

    quiz "A car-glass firm switched from hourly pay to pay per windscreen, and output per worker rose 44%. About half of the rise came from who joined and who stayed. What does the 44% measure?"
        right: Harder work plus a different workforce: about half is the same people working harder, the rest who joined and who stayed.
        wrong: Was it the same people before and after? No, the workforce changed too: here, harder work plus a different workforce.
        * Only harder work by the same people
        + Harder work plus a different workforce
        * Nothing: pay per piece doesn't work

So what happened when markets were allowed in? Families got the last sa (@draw close@)ck back, and the grain followed. Whether a failing market or a failing state costs more is the next fight.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
